var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"networks/#Networks","page":"Networks","text":"A BalancedNetwork is a positive sequence network. Its tables and scalars are properties.\n\nnet = parse(\"case118.m\").value\n\nnet.name                       # \"case118\"\nnet.base_mva                   # 100.0\nnet.base_frequency             # 60.0\n\nnet.buses                      # 118-element Elements{Bus}\nnet.branches                   # Elements{Branch}\nnet.generators                 # Elements{Generator}\nnet.loads                      # Elements{Load}\nnet.shunts                     # Elements{Shunt}\nnet.static_var_compensators\nnet.storage\nnet.switches\nnet.hvdc\nnet.transformers_3w            # Elements{ThreeWindingTransformer}\nnet.areas\n\nEach table is an Elements vector: length, 1-based indexing, iteration, filter, collect, and broadcasting work as on any Julia vector. Every index reads one element from the C library and returns an immutable struct, so collect(net.buses) materializes a table once when it will be read many times.\n\nlength(net.buses)\nnet.buses[1].vm_pu\n[b.id for b in net.buses if b.bus_type == \"REF\"]\nfilter(br -> br.in_service && br.tap_ratio != 0, net.branches)\nsum(g.active_power_max_mw for g in net.generators)","title":"Networks"},{"category":"section","location":"networks/#Element-structs","page":"Networks","text":"Field names follow the C view names in powerio.h, with units in the name: vm_pu, va_degrees, base_kv, resistance_pu, active_power_mw, reactive_power_mvar. An optional field is nothing when the source does not state it.\n\nBus: id (the source bus number), bus_type (\"PQ\", \"PV\", \"REF\", \"ISOLATED\"), voltage and limits, area, zone, name, location.\nBranch: from_bus_id, to_bus_id, series impedance, total and per terminal charging, ratings A, B, C and further named ratings, tap_ratio (the source value; 0 means 1) and effective_tap_ratio, phase_shift_degrees, angle limits, in_service, transformer control, geographic route.\nGenerator: dispatch, limits, voltage_setpoint_pu, machine_base_mva, cost, regulated_bus_id, capabilities, active_power_control.\nLoad and Shunt: one row per element at bus_id; several can share a bus. Shunt.control carries switched shunt blocks.\nStaticVarCompensator, Storage, Switch, Hvdc, ThreeWindingTransformer (with windings and pairwise impedances), Area.\n\nBus references in every table are source bus ids, not positions. Build the map once when positions are needed:\n\nrow = Dict(b.id => k for (k, b) in enumerate(net.buses))\nfrom = [row[br.from_bus_id] for br in net.branches]\n\nAn element's component_id is its local identity; ComponentId(\"load\", load.component_id) names it in an update.","title":"Element structs"},{"category":"section","location":"networks/#Dense-tables","page":"Networks","text":"to_dense returns the tables as dense arrays for code that wants columns: bus_ids, per branch from, to, r, x, b, tap, shift, per generator pg, pmax, and per bus demand and shunt sums. to_graph returns the bus graph as buses and edges. reference_bus_ids lists the \"REF\" buses.","title":"Dense tables"},{"category":"section","location":"networks/#Detailed-connectivity","page":"Networks","text":"Node breaker formats (XIIDM, CGMES) carry substations, voltage levels, terminals, switches, and operational limits beyond the bus branch tables. net.detailed_connectivity is nothing for a bus branch source and a DetailedConnectivity otherwise; its counts property lists the table lengths. The typed tables are not bound in this release.","title":"Detailed connectivity"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.BalancedNetwork","page":"Networks","text":"BalancedNetwork\n\nA positive sequence network: buses, branches, generators, loads, shunts, and the other balanced element tables. Read the tables through properties: net.buses, net.branches, net.generators, net.loads, net.shunts, net.static_var_compensators, net.storage, net.switches, net.hvdc, net.transformers_3w, net.areas. Scalars: net.name, net.base_mva, net.base_frequency, net.geo, net.detailed_connectivity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.BalancedNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Elements","page":"Networks","text":"Elements{T} <: AbstractVector{T}\n\nThe elements of one network table. length, 1-based indexing, iteration, filter, collect, and broadcasting all work; each index fills one C view and converts it to an immutable element struct.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Elements"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Bus","page":"Networks","text":"Bus\n\nOne balanced bus. id is the source bus number; bus_type is \"PQ\", \"PV\", \"REF\", or \"ISOLATED\"; voltages are per unit and degrees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Bus"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Branch","page":"Networks","text":"Branch\n\nOne balanced branch or two winding transformer between from_bus_id and to_bus_id. Impedances and charging are per unit on the system base; total_charging_susceptance_pu is the whole line charging and the four terminal terms give its split; tap_ratio is the source value (0 means 1) and effective_tap_ratio the value in effect; phase_shift_degrees, the angle limits, ratings A, B, C in MVA, further named ratings, optional current_ratings (A, B, C in amperes), the transformer control, and the geographic route.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Branch"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Generator","page":"Networks","text":"Generator\n\nOne balanced generator at bus_id: dispatch, limits, voltage setpoint, machine base, service status, cost, the regulated_bus_id when it differs from the connection bus, capabilities, active_power_control, and the voltage regulation state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Generator"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Load","page":"Networks","text":"Load\n\nOne balanced load at bus_id: p_mw, q_mvar, in_service, and its voltage_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Load"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Shunt","page":"Networks","text":"Shunt\n\nOne balanced shunt at bus_id: conductance_mw and susceptance_mvar at nominal voltage, in_service, the section_count when stated, and the switched shunt control when present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Shunt"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.StaticVarCompensator","page":"Networks","text":"StaticVarCompensator\n\nOne balanced static VAR compensator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.StaticVarCompensator"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Storage","page":"Networks","text":"Storage\n\nOne balanced storage element at bus_id.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Storage"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Switch","page":"Networks","text":"Switch\n\nOne balanced transmission switch between from_bus_id and to_bus_id.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Switch"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Hvdc","page":"Networks","text":"Hvdc\n\nOne balanced two terminal HVDC line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Hvdc"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.ThreeWindingTransformer","page":"Networks","text":"ThreeWindingTransformer\n\nOne balanced three winding transformer: its windings, pairwise impedances, star point voltage, magnetizing branch, and service status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ThreeWindingTransformer"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Area","page":"Networks","text":"Area\n\nOne balanced control area.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Area"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Location","page":"Networks","text":"Location(x, y, kind)\n\nOne point in the network coordinate space. kind names the point kind when the source states one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Location"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.Geo","page":"Networks","text":"Geo\n\nCoordinate metadata of a network: the coordinate space, its crs and kind when stated, and the canvas size (canvas_width, canvas_height, canvas_units) for display coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Geo"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.ComponentId","page":"Networks","text":"ComponentId(component_type, local_id)\n\nStable identity of one component: its structural type (\"bus\", \"load\", \"generator\", \"branch\", \"switch\", ...) and its local identity. An element's component_id field is the local identity, so ComponentId(\"load\", load.component_id) names that load in an update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ComponentId"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.TerminalReference","page":"Networks","text":"TerminalReference(equipment, terminal)\n\nA numbered terminal of one piece of equipment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.TerminalReference"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.LoadVoltageModel","page":"Networks","text":"LoadVoltageModel\n\nVoltage dependence of one load: kind names the model; the constant power, constant current, and constant impedance shares and the exponential terms follow the C view field for field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.LoadVoltageModel"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.ShuntBlock","page":"Networks","text":"ShuntBlock(steps, conductance_mw, susceptance_mvar)\n\nOne block of a switched shunt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ShuntBlock"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.ShuntControl","page":"Networks","text":"ShuntControl\n\nSwitched shunt control: mode, the voltage band vmax_pu and vmin_pu, the controlled bus_id when it differs from the shunt's bus, the reactive range percent, and the switchable blocks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ShuntControl"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.TransformerControl","page":"Networks","text":"TransformerControl\n\nAutomatic tap or phase control of a transformer branch or winding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.TransformerControl"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.BranchRating","page":"Networks","text":"BranchRating(name, rate_mva)\n\nOne named MVA rating of a branch beyond ratings A, B, and C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.BranchRating"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.GeneratorCost","page":"Networks","text":"GeneratorCost\n\nOne MATPOWER style cost curve: model (1 piecewise linear, 2 polynomial), startup and shutdown costs, ncost, and the coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.GeneratorCost"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.GeneratorCapability","page":"Networks","text":"GeneratorCapability(name, value)\n\nOne optional generator capability or ramp field; value is nothing when the source leaves it unset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.GeneratorCapability"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.ActivePowerControl","page":"Networks","text":"ActivePowerControl\n\nGovernor and distributed slack settings of a generator or storage element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ActivePowerControl"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.HvdcConverter","page":"Networks","text":"HvdcConverter\n\nOne AC terminal converter station of an HVDC line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.HvdcConverter"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.TransformerWinding","page":"Networks","text":"TransformerWinding\n\nOne winding of a three winding transformer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.TransformerWinding"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.TransformerImpedance","page":"Networks","text":"TransformerImpedance(resistance_pu, reactance_pu, base_mva)\n\nOne pairwise impedance of a three winding transformer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.TransformerImpedance"},{"category":"type","location":"networks/#PowerIO.DetailedConnectivity","page":"Networks","text":"DetailedConnectivity\n\nSource neutral detailed connectivity retained from node breaker formats such as XIIDM and CGMES. dc.counts is a NamedTuple of table lengths. The typed tables are not bound in this release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.DetailedConnectivity"},{"category":"function","location":"networks/#PowerIO.reference_bus_ids","page":"Networks","text":"reference_bus_ids(net::BalancedNetwork) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe ids of the buses whose type is \"REF\", in table order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.reference_bus_ids"},{"category":"function","location":"networks/#PowerIO.to_dense","page":"Networks","text":"to_dense(net::BalancedNetwork) -> NamedTuple\nto_dense(m::PioModule{BalancedNetwork}) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe balanced tables as dense arrays. Fields:\n\nn, m, ng, ns: bus, branch, generator, and switch counts.\nbase_mva: system base.\nbus_ids::Vector{Int}: source bus ids in table order; row k of every per bus table is bus bus_ids[k].\nbranch: from, to (bus ids), r, x, b (per unit; b is the total charging), g_fr, b_fr, g_to, b_to (terminal split), tap (source value, 0 means 1), shift (degrees), in_service::Vector{Bool}.\ngen: bus, pg, qg, pmax, pmin, qmax, qmin (MW and MVAr), vg (per unit), mbase (MVA), in_service.\ndemand, shunt: per bus sums of in-service loads (pd, qd) and shunts (gs, bs), in MW and MVAr at nominal voltage.\nreference_bus: the 1-based row of the single \"REF\" bus, or nothing when there is none or several.\nswitch: from, to, closed::Vector{Bool}, thermal_rating, current_rating, pf, qf, pt, qt (absent optionals are 0.0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_dense"},{"category":"function","location":"networks/#PowerIO.to_graph","page":"Networks","text":"to_graph(net::BalancedNetwork) -> NamedTuple\nto_graph(net::MulticonductorNetwork) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe bus graph of a network. buses lists every bus with its id, 1-based index in the bus table, and for balanced networks its kind and name. edges lists the connections that carry power: in-service branches for a balanced network; lines, closed switches, and transformer winding pairs for a multiconductor network. Each edge has a kind, its 1-based index in the source table, and from and to bus ids. Parallel elements stay separate edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_graph"},{"category":"section","location":"collections/#Collections-and-instances","page":"Collections and instances","text":"","title":"Collections and instances"},{"category":"section","location":"collections/#Time-series-and-scenario-sets","page":"Collections and instances","text":"A PyPSA folder with several snapshots parses into a TimeSeries{BalancedNetwork}; a GridFM dataset parses into a ScenarioSet{BalancedNetwork}. Both hold typed values and follow Julia's collection conventions.\n\nseries = parse(\"pypsa_folder/\").value    # TimeSeries{BalancedNetwork}\nlength(series)\nseries[1]                                # BalancedNetwork, 1-based\nfor net in series\n    sum(l.p_mw for l in net.loads)\nend\n\nscenarios = parse(\"gridfm_case14/\").value  # ScenarioSet{BalancedNetwork}\nkeys(scenarios)                            # [\"0\", \"1\", ...]\nscenarios[\"0\"]                             # BalancedNetwork\nhaskey(scenarios, \"7\")\nfor (id, net) in scenarios\n    println(id, \": \", length(net.buses), \" buses\")\nend\n\nEntries are read from the collection without copying a network. An OperatingPoint exposes its network.","title":"Time series and scenario sets"},{"category":"section","location":"collections/#Calculation-instances-and-solutions","page":"Collections and instances","text":"A calculation instance pairs a network with the specification of one calculation. instance.network is the network. Construct one from a network module:\n\ncase = parse(\"case9.m\")\nopf = to_dc_opf_instance(case)           # PioModule{DcOpfInstance}\nopf.value.network                        # the BalancedNetwork\nopf.history[1].name                      # \"to_dc_opf_instance\"\nemit(opf, \"matpower\")                    # writes the network; diagnoses the rest\nserialize(opf, \"case9_dcopf.pio.json\")\n\nConstructor Result\nto_dc_pf_instance, to_ac_pf_instance DcPfInstance, AcPfInstance\nto_dc_opf_instance, to_ac_opf_instance DcOpfInstance, AcOpfInstance\nto_mc_ac_pf_instance, to_mc_ac_opf_instance McAcPfInstance, McAcOpfInstance over a MulticonductorNetwork\n\nA solution answers an instance. solution.instance is that instance, solution.termination the solver status (\"converged\", \"iteration_limit\", \"infeasible\", \"unbounded\", \"failed\", \"not_reported\"), and solution.objective the reported objective or nothing. Named quantities are read by name, one Vector{Float64} in table order:\n\nsolution = parse(\"example_0.json\").value  # AcOpfSolution from OPFData\nsolution.instance.network\nsolution[\"bus_voltage_magnitude\"]\nsolution[\"bus_voltage_angle\"]\nsolution[\"generator_active_power\"]\nsolution[\"branch_from_active_flow\"]\n\nscuc = parse(\"scenario_002/\").value       # AcScucSolution from a GO Challenge 3 pair\ntime_count(scuc)\nscuc[\"bus_voltage_magnitude\", 1]          # time position 1\n\nAn unknown quantity name throws PowerIOError with code REQUEST.CAPI.QUANTITY_UNKNOWN. SocwrOpfSolution records a second order cone relaxation of an AC OPF instance; it reports objective_lower_bound and is not an AC feasible point.","title":"Calculation instances and solutions"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.TimeSeries","page":"Collections and instances","text":"TimeSeries{T}\n\nValues of type T in chronological order. Supports length, 1-based getindex, and iteration over the values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.TimeSeries"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.ScenarioSet","page":"Collections and instances","text":"ScenarioSet{T}\n\nValues of type T keyed by scenario identifier. Supports length, keys, values, haskey, getindex with a scenario id, and iteration over id => value pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ScenarioSet"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.OperatingPoint","page":"Collections and instances","text":"OperatingPoint{N}\n\nOne operating point over a network of type N. point.network is that network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.OperatingPoint"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.DcPfInstance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"DcPfInstance\n\nDC power flow instance over a BalancedNetwork. instance.network is the network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.DcPfInstance"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.AcPfInstance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"AcPfInstance\n\nAC power flow instance over a BalancedNetwork. instance.network is the network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.AcPfInstance"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.DcOpfInstance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"DcOpfInstance\n\nDC optimal power flow instance over a BalancedNetwork. instance.network is the network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.DcOpfInstance"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.AcOpfInstance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"AcOpfInstance\n\nAC optimal power flow instance over a BalancedNetwork. instance.network is the network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.AcOpfInstance"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.McAcPfInstance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"McAcPfInstance\n\nMulticonductor AC power flow instance over a MulticonductorNetwork. instance.network is the network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.McAcPfInstance"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.McAcOpfInstance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"McAcOpfInstance\n\nMulticonductor AC optimal power flow instance over a MulticonductorNetwork. instance.network is the network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.McAcOpfInstance"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.AcScucInstance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"AcScucInstance\n\nAC security constrained unit commitment instance over a BalancedNetwork. instance.network is the network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.AcScucInstance"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.DcPfSolution","page":"Collections and instances","text":"DcPfSolution\n\nDC power flow solution. solution.instance is the instance it answers and solution.termination the solver status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.DcPfSolution"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.AcPfSolution","page":"Collections and instances","text":"AcPfSolution\n\nAC power flow solution. solution.instance is the instance it answers and solution.termination the solver status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.AcPfSolution"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.DcOpfSolution","page":"Collections and instances","text":"DcOpfSolution\n\nDC optimal power flow solution. solution.instance is the instance it answers and solution.termination the solver status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.DcOpfSolution"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.AcOpfSolution","page":"Collections and instances","text":"AcOpfSolution\n\nAC optimal power flow solution. solution.instance is the instance it answers and solution.termination the solver status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.AcOpfSolution"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.SocwrOpfSolution","page":"Collections and instances","text":"SocwrOpfSolution\n\nSecond order cone (SOCWR) relaxation of an AC OPF instance; not an AC feasible point. solution.instance is the instance it answers and solution.termination the solver status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.SocwrOpfSolution"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.McAcPfSolution","page":"Collections and instances","text":"McAcPfSolution\n\nMulticonductor AC power flow solution. solution.instance is the instance it answers and solution.termination the solver status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.McAcPfSolution"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.McAcOpfSolution","page":"Collections and instances","text":"McAcOpfSolution\n\nMulticonductor AC optimal power flow solution. solution.instance is the instance it answers and solution.termination the solver status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.McAcOpfSolution"},{"category":"type","location":"collections/#PowerIO.AcScucSolution","page":"Collections and instances","text":"AcScucSolution\n\nAC security constrained unit commitment solution. solution.instance is the instance it answers and solution.termination the solver status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.AcScucSolution"},{"category":"function","location":"collections/#PowerIO.time_count","page":"Collections and instances","text":"time_count(solution::AcScucSolution) -> Int\n\nThe number of time positions in an AC SCUC solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.time_count"},{"category":"function","location":"collections/#PowerIO.to_dc_pf_instance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"to_dc_pf_instance(m::PioModule) -> PioModule\n\nConstruct a DC power flow instance from a network module. The network is shared, not copied; the new module records the construction in its history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_dc_pf_instance"},{"category":"function","location":"collections/#PowerIO.to_ac_pf_instance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"to_ac_pf_instance(m::PioModule) -> PioModule\n\nConstruct an AC power flow instance from a network module. The network is shared, not copied; the new module records the construction in its history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_ac_pf_instance"},{"category":"function","location":"collections/#PowerIO.to_dc_opf_instance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"to_dc_opf_instance(m::PioModule) -> PioModule\n\nConstruct a DC optimal power flow instance from a network module. The network is shared, not copied; the new module records the construction in its history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_dc_opf_instance"},{"category":"function","location":"collections/#PowerIO.to_ac_opf_instance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"to_ac_opf_instance(m::PioModule) -> PioModule\n\nConstruct an AC optimal power flow instance from a network module. The network is shared, not copied; the new module records the construction in its history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_ac_opf_instance"},{"category":"function","location":"collections/#PowerIO.to_mc_ac_pf_instance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"to_mc_ac_pf_instance(m::PioModule) -> PioModule\n\nConstruct a multiconductor AC power flow instance from a network module. The network is shared, not copied; the new module records the construction in its history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_mc_ac_pf_instance"},{"category":"function","location":"collections/#PowerIO.to_mc_ac_opf_instance","page":"Collections and instances","text":"to_mc_ac_opf_instance(m::PioModule) -> PioModule\n\nConstruct a multiconductor AC optimal power flow instance from a network module. The network is shared, not copied; the new module records the construction in its history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_mc_ac_opf_instance"},{"category":"section","location":"distribution/#Distribution-networks","page":"Distribution","text":"OpenDSS, PMD JSON, and BMOPF sources parse into a MulticonductorNetwork: buses with named terminals, lines with per length impedance matrices, transformers with windings, loads and generators with per terminal powers.\n\nfeeder = parse(\"IEEE13Nodeckt.dss\")     # PioModule{MulticonductorNetwork}\nnet = feeder.value\n\nnet.name\nnet.base_frequency                       # 60.0\nnet.source_format                        # \"dss\"\n\nnet.buses                                # Elements{MulticonductorBus}\nnet.linecodes                            # Elements{MulticonductorLineCode}\nnet.lines\nnet.switches\nnet.transformers\nnet.loads\nnet.generators\nnet.ibrs                                 # inverter based resources\nnet.control_profiles\nnet.shunts\nnet.capacitors\nnet.voltage_sources\nnet.untyped                              # source objects kept without a typed slot\nnet.commands                             # retained source commands (solve, ...)\nnet.options                              # name => value pairs\n\nlc = net.linecodes[1]\nlc.resistance                            # conductor_count square, ohm per metre\nlc.reactance\nlc.susceptance_from + lc.susceptance_to  # siemens per metre\n\nline = net.lines[1]\nline.bus_from, line.terminals_from       # \"650\", [\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"]\nline.line_code, line.length_m\n\nload = net.loads[1]\nload.terminals                           # phases and neutral\nload.active_power_nominal_w              # one entry per phase\nload.voltage_model                       # \"constant_power\", ...\n\nUnits are SI: volts, watts, vars, metres, ohms, siemens. Terminal maps are vectors of terminal names in the order the source lists them.","title":"Distribution networks"},{"category":"section","location":"distribution/#Writing","page":"Distribution","text":"emit writes the same three formats. A module read from OpenDSS writes its original .dss files unchanged when nothing changed; PMD JSON and BMOPF are canonical output with diagnostics for whatever the target cannot carry.\n\nemit(feeder, \"dss\", \"copy.dss\")\nemit(feeder, \"pmd\").text\nemit(feeder, \"bmopf\").text","title":"Writing"},{"category":"section","location":"distribution/#PowerIO-IR-and-grid-exchange-formats","page":"Distribution","text":"OpenDSS, PMD JSON, and BMOPF are grid exchange formats: other tools read and write them, and they enter PowerIO through parse and leave through emit. PowerIO IR (serialize, deserialize) is PowerIO's own serialization of a module, for passing a module with its diagnostics and history to another PowerIO consumer. It is not an exchange format and no other tool reads it.","title":"PowerIO IR and grid exchange formats"},{"category":"section","location":"distribution/#Calculations","page":"Distribution","text":"to_mc_ac_pf_instance and to_mc_ac_opf_instance construct multiconductor power flow and optimal power flow instances from a network module. The multiconductor admittance matrix is not bound in this release.","title":"Calculations"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorNetwork","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorNetwork\n\nA conductor level distribution network. Read the tables through properties: net.buses, net.linecodes, net.lines, net.switches, net.transformers, net.loads, net.generators, net.ibrs, net.control_profiles, net.shunts, net.capacitors, net.voltage_sources, net.untyped, net.commands, net.options. Scalars: net.name, net.base_frequency, net.source_format, net.geo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorBus","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorBus\n\nOne multiconductor bus: its id, terminals, grounded_terminals, and the voltage limits the source states (volts; per terminal vectors for the phase limits).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorBus"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorLineCode","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorLineCode\n\nPer length impedance of a line: the resistance, reactance, and terminal conductance_from, susceptance_from, conductance_to, susceptance_to matrices (ohm per metre and siemens per metre, conductor_count square), the per conductor limits, and the source the code came from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorLineCode"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorLine","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorLine\n\nOne line from bus_from to bus_to with its terminal maps, line_code, length_m, optional route, and per conductor limits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorLine"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorSwitch","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorSwitch\n\nOne switch between bus_from and bus_to; open is its state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorSwitch"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorTransformer","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorTransformer\n\nOne transformer: its windings, the pairwise short_circuit_reactance_percent values, and phase_count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorTransformer"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorTransformerWinding","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorTransformerWinding\n\nOne winding: its bus, terminals, connection (wye or delta), ratings, resistance_percent, tap, and neutral impedance when grounded through one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorTransformerWinding"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorLoad","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorLoad\n\nOne load: configuration, per terminal nominal powers, the voltage_model name, and the per terminal ZIP and exponential coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorLoad"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorGenerator","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorGenerator\n\nOne generator with per terminal nominal powers, optional limits, dispatch cost, and apparent power and current limits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorGenerator"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.InverterBasedResource","page":"Distribution","text":"InverterBasedResource\n\nOne inverter based resource: topology, prime_mover, limits, available active power, and the control_profile it follows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.InverterBasedResource"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.ControlProfile","page":"Distribution","text":"ControlProfile\n\nOne inverter control profile: a fixed power_factor, a volt_var curve, a volt_watt curve, or a combination.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ControlProfile"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.VoltVarControl","page":"Distribution","text":"VoltVarControl\n\nVolt-var curve of a control profile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.VoltVarControl"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.VoltWattControl","page":"Distribution","text":"VoltWattControl\n\nVolt-watt curve of a control profile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.VoltWattControl"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorShunt","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorShunt\n\nOne shunt with its conductance and susceptance matrices (siemens).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorShunt"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.MulticonductorCapacitor","page":"Distribution","text":"MulticonductorCapacitor\n\nOne rated capacitor bank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorCapacitor"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.VoltageSource","page":"Distribution","text":"VoltageSource\n\nOne ideal voltage source with per terminal magnitude (volts) and angle (radians).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.VoltageSource"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.UntypedObject","page":"Distribution","text":"UntypedObject\n\nOne source object retained without a typed PowerIO representation: its class_name, name, and properties as name => value pairs (a positional property has nothing as its name).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.UntypedObject"},{"category":"type","location":"distribution/#PowerIO.SourceCommand","page":"Distribution","text":"SourceCommand(verb, args)\n\nOne retained source command, such as an OpenDSS solve line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.SourceCommand"},{"category":"section","location":"modules/#Modules","page":"Modules","text":"Every source parses into a PioModule: one typed value together with the records that describe how it was produced.\n\ncase = parse(\"case118.m\")\ncase isa PioModule{BalancedNetwork}    # true\ncase.value                              # the BalancedNetwork\ncase.diagnostics                        # Vector{Diagnostic}\ncase.producer                           # Producer(\"powerio\", \"0.11.0\")\ncase.sources                            # the files it was read from\ncase.history                            # the operations applied so far\n\nThe type parameter follows the source. Dispatch on it:\n\nsummarize(m::PioModule{BalancedNetwork}) = length(m.value.buses)\nsummarize(m::PioModule{MulticonductorNetwork}) = length(m.value.lines)\nsummarize(m::PioModule{<:TimeSeries}) = length(m.value)\n\nSource Value type\nMATPOWER, PSS/E RAW and RAWX, XIIDM, CGMES, PowerWorld, PSLF EPC, PowerModels JSON, Egret JSON, pandapower JSON, PyPSA CSV (one snapshot) BalancedNetwork\nOpenDSS, PMD JSON, BMOPF MulticonductorNetwork\nPyPSA CSV with several snapshots TimeSeries{BalancedNetwork}\nGridFM Parquet ScenarioSet{BalancedNetwork}\nGO Challenge 3 problem, or problem and solution AcScucInstance, AcScucSolution\nOPFData AcOpfSolution\n\nA value type this release does not bind arrives as UnknownValue with its structural type name.","title":"Modules"},{"category":"section","location":"modules/#Sources","page":"Modules","text":"parse takes a path, an IO, or bytes. format is a canonical token such as \"matpower\", \"psse\", \"xiidm\", \"cgmes\", or \"dss\"; when omitted, the source name and content select it. name supplies the source name for an in-memory source.\n\nparse(\"case9.m\")\nparse(\"cgmes_case/\")                                    # a directory or ZIP for a profile set\nparse(IOBuffer(text); format=\"matpower\", name=\"case9.m\")\nparse(bytes; format=\"pwb\", name=\"case.pwb\")\nopen(\"case9.m\") do io\n    parse(io)                                           # an open file keeps its path as the name\nend\n\nA failed parse throws PowerIOError with a stable code, the rendered message, and the structured diagnostics that caused it. Branch on the code, not the message.","title":"Sources"},{"category":"section","location":"modules/#Diagnostics","page":"Modules","text":"Diagnostics belong to the module, not to the value. Each Diagnostic has a code (\"READ.MATPOWER.FIELD_DEFAULTED\", \"EMIT.PSSE.FIELD_DROPPED\"), a severity (:error, :warning, :remark, :note), a message, and when the reader recorded them an id, a target naming the element concerned, a suggested_action, source spans, related diagnostic ids, and structured details.\n\nfor d in case.diagnostics\n    d.severity == :warning || continue\n    println(d.code, \": \", d.message)\nend","title":"Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"modules/#Emit","page":"Modules","text":"emit writes a module as a grid exchange format and returns an EmitResult.\n\nsame = emit(case, \"matpower\")            # in memory\nsame.fidelity                            # \"exact_same_format\"\nsame.text                                # the original file content\n\nother = emit(case, \"psse\")\nother.fidelity                           # \"canonical\": freshly written\nother.diagnostics                        # what PSS/E cannot carry\n\nemit(case, \"psse\", \"case.raw\")           # one file on disk\nemit(case, \"pypsa-csv\", \"case_dir\")      # a directory of files\nemit(case, \"matpower\", stdout)           # a writable IO receives the single file\n\nresult.artifacts lists what was produced, one Artifact per file with its name and either its data (in memory) or its path (on disk). layout is \"file\" or \"directory\". fidelity is \"exact_same_format\" when the module was read from that format and its value is unchanged, so the output is the original file content; otherwise \"canonical\". text is the content of the single in-memory UTF-8 file, or nothing.","title":"Emit"},{"category":"section","location":"modules/#PowerIO-IR","page":"Modules","text":"PowerIO IR is PowerIO's own serialization of a module: one JSON document (\"schema\": \"powerio.module\", and a \"version\" naming the PowerIO release that wrote it) holding the typed value with its diagnostics, producer, sources, source mappings, history, and extensions. serialize writes it and deserialize reads it.\n\nA library reads the documents of its own compatible release line no newer than itself: PowerIO 0.11.3 reads 0.11.0 through 0.11.3, and refuses 0.10.0, 0.12.0, and 0.11.4 with the version it found named. library_version reports this library's own version. A refused document says which remedy applies: a later release needs a newer PowerIO, and an earlier one has to be regenerated from its original power system data.\n\nserialize(case, \"case9.pio.json\")\nback = deserialize(\"case9.pio.json\")     # PioModule{BalancedNetwork}\n\nPowerIO IR is not a grid exchange format. parse does not read it and emit does not write it, and it does not appear in format discovery. Use it when both sides are PowerIO consumers and the module records matter; use emit for every other tool. The document does not carry the original file content, so a deserialized module writes canonical output rather than the original file.","title":"PowerIO IR"},{"category":"section","location":"modules/#Constructions","page":"Modules","text":"to_dc_pf_instance, to_ac_pf_instance, to_dc_opf_instance, to_ac_opf_instance, to_mc_ac_pf_instance, and to_mc_ac_opf_instance construct a calculation instance module from a network module. The network is shared, and the new module records the construction in its history. See Collections and instances.","title":"Constructions"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.PioModule","page":"Modules","text":"PioModule{T}\n\nOne typed value together with the records that describe how it was produced.\n\nm.value::T: the value. Dispatch on PioModule{BalancedNetwork}, PioModule{MulticonductorNetwork}, PioModule{TimeSeries{...}}, and the other concrete parameters.\nm.diagnostics::Vector{Diagnostic}: the findings stored on the module.\nm.producer::Producer: the program that produced the module.\nm.sources::Vector{ModuleSource}: the sources it was read from.\nm.history::Vector{HistoryEntry}: the operations applied so far.\n\nparse and deserialize return modules. emit and serialize consume them. apply_updates! changes the value in place and refreshes m.value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.PioModule"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.Producer","page":"Modules","text":"Producer(name, version)\n\nThe program identity recorded with a module.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Producer"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.ModuleSource","page":"Modules","text":"ModuleSource\n\nOne source recorded with a module: its id, name, byte_length, the format token when the reader recorded one, and the digest_algorithm and digest when a digest was recorded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ModuleSource"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.HistoryEntry","page":"Modules","text":"HistoryEntry\n\nOne operation recorded in module history: its id, kind, name, the input_type and output_type structural names when recorded, the named parameters as a Dict{String,String} of parameter name to value kind, and the assumptions and losses the operation declared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.HistoryEntry"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.Diagnostic","page":"Modules","text":"Diagnostic\n\nOne finding recorded by a reader, writer, or transformation.\n\ncode: the stable code to branch on, such as \"READ.MATPOWER.FIELD_DEFAULTED\".\nseverity: :error, :warning, :remark, or :note.\nmessage: rendered text for people.\nid: durable identity, or nothing.\ntarget: locator of the value element concerned, or nothing.\nsuggested_action: what to do about it, or nothing.\nspans: source byte ranges.\nrelated: identities of related diagnostics.\ndetails: structured details as a JSON3.Object, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Diagnostic"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.SourceSpan","page":"Modules","text":"SourceSpan(source, byte_start, byte_end)\n\nOne byte range in a named source, attached to a Diagnostic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.SourceSpan"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.PowerIOError","page":"Modules","text":"PowerIOError(code, message, diagnostics)\n\nA failure reported by the PowerIO library. code is the stable diagnostic code to branch on, message is the rendered text, and diagnostics are the structured Diagnostic records that caused the failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.PowerIOError"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.UnknownValue","page":"Modules","text":"UnknownValue\n\nA module value whose structural type name this PowerIO.jl release does not bind. value.type_name is the name the library reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.UnknownValue"},{"category":"function","location":"modules/#PowerIO.emit","page":"Modules","text":"emit(m::PioModule, format, destination=nothing) -> EmitResult\n\nWrite the module's value as the grid exchange format named by format (\"matpower\", \"psse\", \"xiidm\", \"cgmes\", \"dss\", \"pmd\", \"bmopf\", \"powermodels-json\", \"pypsa-csv\", and the other canonical tokens). With destination === nothing the files stay in memory. A path writes one file or a directory. A writable IO receives the single file.\n\nWhen the module was read from the same format and its value is unchanged, the output is the original file content (fidelity == \"exact_same_format\"). Anything the target cannot carry is reported in result.diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.emit"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.EmitResult","page":"Modules","text":"EmitResult\n\nWhat one emission produced.\n\nartifacts::Vector{Artifact}: one entry per file.\nlayout::String: \"file\" or \"directory\".\nfidelity::String: \"exact_same_format\" when the output is the original file content the reader kept, \"canonical\" for freshly written output.\ndiagnostics::Vector{Diagnostic}: what the writer kept, defaulted, or could not carry.\ntext: the content of the one in-memory UTF-8 file as a String, or nothing when the result holds anything else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.EmitResult"},{"category":"type","location":"modules/#PowerIO.Artifact","page":"Modules","text":"Artifact\n\nOne file produced by emit or serialize; the Rust, Python, and C APIs use the same name. name is the file name. data holds the content after an in-memory emission and is nothing after a write to disk; path holds the written path after a write to disk and is nothing for an in-memory emission.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.Artifact"},{"category":"function","location":"modules/#PowerIO.serialize","page":"Modules","text":"serialize(m::PioModule, destination=nothing) -> EmitResult\n\nWrite the module as PowerIO IR: one JSON document holding the typed value with its diagnostics, producer, sources, source mappings, history, and extensions. Read it back with deserialize. PowerIO IR carries PowerIO values between PowerIO consumers; use emit for other tools.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.serialize"},{"category":"function","location":"modules/#PowerIO.deserialize","page":"Modules","text":"deserialize(path::AbstractString) -> PioModule\ndeserialize(io::IO) -> PioModule\ndeserialize(bytes::AbstractVector{UInt8}) -> PioModule\n\nRead one PowerIO IR document written by serialize: \"schema\": \"powerio.module\" and a \"version\" naming the PowerIO release that wrote it. The library reads the documents of its own compatible release line no newer than itself and refuses any other, naming the version it found. PowerIO IR is not a grid exchange format; parse does not accept it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.deserialize"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"Every exported name, grouped by source file. The guides link to these entries.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#The-package","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"The package"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Parsing","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Parsing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Display-and-identity","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Display and identity"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Everything-else","page":"API reference","text":"The remaining docstrings appear on their guide pages: Modules, Networks, Distribution, Collections and instances, Matrices, Updates, Interop, and Binary distribution.\n\n","title":"Everything else"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#PowerIO","page":"API reference","text":"PowerIO\n\nJulia binding of PowerIO, the power system data compiler, over C ABI 7.\n\nusing PowerIO\ncase = parse(\"case9.m\")             # PioModule{BalancedNetwork}\nnet = case.value\nlength(net.buses)                   # 9\nnet.branches[1].reactance_pu\ncase.diagnostics                    # the reader's findings\nemit(case, \"matpower\", \"copy.m\")    # same format: writes the original file unchanged\nresult = emit(case, \"psse\")         # another format, in memory\nresult.text\nserialize(case, \"case9.pio.json\")   # PowerIO IR\n\nparse reads a grid exchange source into a typed module. emit writes one. serialize and deserialize move PowerIO IR between PowerIO consumers. calc_* functions compute matrices and vectors. to_* functions construct another value type in memory. apply_updates! changes a module in place.\n\nThe C library resolves automatically: the bundled artifact, or a sibling powerio build during development. Point at a custom build with set_library!, the POWERIO_CAPI environment variable, or a persisted Preferences.jl override.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.parse-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API reference","text":"parse(path::AbstractString; format=nothing) -> PioModule\nparse(io::IO; format=nothing, name=nothing) -> PioModule\nparse(bytes::AbstractVector{UInt8}; format=nothing, name=\"<memory>\") -> PioModule\n\nParse one grid exchange source into a typed module. A string is a path to a file or directory. An IO or byte vector is read in memory; name supplies the source name for format detection and diagnostics (an open file stream uses its path). format is a canonical format token such as \"matpower\", \"psse\", or \"dss\"; when omitted, the source name and content select the format.\n\nThe value type follows the source: MATPOWER, PSS/E, XIIDM, CGMES, and the other balanced formats give PioModule{BalancedNetwork}; OpenDSS, PMD JSON, and BMOPF give PioModule{MulticonductorNetwork}; PyPSA and GridFM folders give time series and scenario sets; GO Challenge 3 and OPFData give calculation instances and solutions.\n\nThese methods extend Base.parse so the bare name works after using PowerIO. Parse failures throw PowerIOError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.parse"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.ActivePowerHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Active power with an explicit unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ActivePowerHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.ApparentPowerHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Apparent power with an explicit unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ApparentPowerHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.ArtifactHandle","page":"API reference","text":"One emitted artifact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ArtifactHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.BalancedNetworkHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Balanced network; keeps its module alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.BalancedNetworkHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.CalculationInstanceHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Calculation instance; keeps its module alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.CalculationInstanceHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.CalculationSolutionHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Calculation solution; keeps its module alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.CalculationSolutionHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.CalculationUpdateHandle","page":"API reference","text":"One update ready to apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.CalculationUpdateHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.ComponentIdHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Stable component identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ComponentIdHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.DestinationHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Selected output location.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.DestinationHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.DetailedConnectivityHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Detailed connectivity tables; keep their network alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.DetailedConnectivityHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.DiagnosticsHandle","page":"API reference","text":"One diagnostics list.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.DiagnosticsHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.EmitResultHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Completed emission inventory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.EmitResultHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.JsonValueHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Owned structured JSON value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.JsonValueHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.ModuleHandle","page":"API reference","text":"One PioModule inside the C library.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ModuleHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.MulticonductorNetworkHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Multiconductor network; keeps its module alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.MulticonductorNetworkHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.NetworkUpdateHandle","page":"API reference","text":"One network update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.NetworkUpdateHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.OperatingPointHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Operating point; keeps its module alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.OperatingPointHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.OperatingPointUpdateHandle","page":"API reference","text":"One operating point update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.OperatingPointUpdateHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.ReactivePowerHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Reactive power with an explicit unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ReactivePowerHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.ScenarioSetHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Scenario set; keeps its module alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ScenarioSetHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.SourceHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Acquired input bytes or path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.SourceHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.SparseMatrixHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Owned CSR matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.SparseMatrixHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.StringHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Owned string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.StringHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.TimeSeriesHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Time series; keeps its module alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.TimeSeriesHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.UpdateChangeHandle","page":"API reference","text":"One change from an update report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.UpdateChangeHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.UpdateReportHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Report of one applied update batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.UpdateReportHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.ValueHandle","page":"API reference","text":"View of a module value; keeps the module alive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ValueHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PowerIO.VectorHandle","page":"API reference","text":"Owned double vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.VectorHandle"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-0.11/#Migrating-from-0.10-to-0.11","page":"Migrating from 0.10 to 0.11","text":"PowerIO.jl 0.11 binds PowerIO 0.11 over C ABI 7. Element tables are properties that return typed Julia structs, the operations follow the cross language vocabulary (parse, emit, serialize, deserialize, calc_*, to_*, apply_updates!), and everything the 0.10 binding read from a network's JSON payload now comes from typed C views.","title":"Migrating from 0.10 to 0.11"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-0.11/#Parsing-and-writing","page":"Migrating from 0.10 to 0.11","text":"0.10 0.11\nparse_file(path; format) parse(path; format)\nparse_text(text; name, format) parse(IOBuffer(text); name, format) or parse(codeunits(text); ...)\nparse_file(BalancedNetwork, path) parse(path).value\nkind(m) dispatch on PioModule{T} or typeof(m.value)\ninspect(m), source_format(m) m.sources, m.history, m.producer\nemit(m, format) returning text emit(m, format) returning an EmitResult; result.text, result.artifacts\nparse_bytes(bytes; name, format) parse(bytes; name, format)\nwrite_file(m, path[; format]) emit(m, format, path), or emit(m, m.sources[1].format, path) for the source format\nto_json(m), from_json(PioModule, text) serialize(m), deserialize(bytes); a stored 0.10 document is refused and must be regenerated from its original power system data\nto_json(net), from_json(text) removed: a network is written through its module\nemit(m, \"pio-json\", path) serialize(m, path)\nresolve_format, FormatInfo removed: format discovery is not part of the C ABI","title":"Parsing and writing"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-0.11/#Network-access","page":"Migrating from 0.10 to 0.11","text":"0.10 0.11\nbuses(net), branches(net), generators(net), loads(net), shunts(net), storage(net), hvdc(net), switches(net) net.buses, net.branches, net.generators, net.loads, net.shunts, net.storage, net.hvdc, net.switches\nlines(net), linecodes(net), transformers(net), ibrs(net), control_profiles(net), capacitors(net), voltage_sources(net), untyped(net) net.lines, net.linecodes, net.transformers, net.ibrs, net.control_profiles, net.capacitors, net.voltage_sources, net.untyped\nn_buses(net), n_branches(net), n_generators(net), n_switches(net) length(net.buses), length(net.branches), ...\nbase_mva(net), base_frequency(net), network_name(net) net.base_mva, net.base_frequency, net.name\nnet.data.buses[1].vm (a JSON3.Object) net.buses[1].vm_pu (a Bus)\nreference_bus_id(net), reference_bus_positions(net) reference_bus_ids(net)\nn_islands(net), is_radial(net) removed: not part of the C ABI\nto_bus_type_code(kind) bus.bus_type is \"PQ\", \"PV\", \"REF\", or \"ISOLATED\"\nto_normalized(net) removed: not part of the C ABI\nto_arrow, ArrowTable, arrow_catalog removed: ABI 7 has no Arrow export; use the element collections or to_dense\nto_dense(net) kept, assembled from the element collections; no n_components or is_radial fields\n\nElement struct fields use the C view names: vm_pu for vm, va_degrees for va, resistance_pu for r, reactance_pu for x, total_charging_susceptance_pu for b, active_power_mw for pg, from_bus_id for from, tap_ratio for tap, phase_shift_degrees for shift.","title":"Network access"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-0.11/#Collections-and-calculations","page":"Migrating from 0.10 to 0.11","text":"0.10 0.11\nlist_states(m), export_state(m; time=i) series[i] on a TimeSeries, scenarios[id] on a ScenarioSet\nto_balanced(m), to_balanced_report(m) removed: not part of the C ABI\ncalc_branch_susceptance_matrix calc_branch_flow_matrix\ncalc_phase_shift_injection calc_branch_phase_shift_injection and calc_bus_phase_shift_injection\ncalc_admittance_matrix, calc_bprime_matrix, calc_bdoubleprime_matrix kept; assembled in Julia from the element tables\nhistory(m), module_sources(m) m.history, m.sources","title":"Collections and calculations"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-0.11/#Feature-probes","page":"Migrating from 0.10 to 0.11","text":"0.10 0.11\nfeatures(), has_feature, build_info, schema_versions removed: ABI 7 declares one fixed symbol table\narrow_available, matrix_available, gridfm_available, dist_available, prob_available removed; a build without gridfm reports a coded parse error\nabi_version() == 6 abi_version() == 7","title":"Feature probes"},{"category":"section","location":"migration-0.11/#New-in-0.11","page":"Migrating from 0.10 to 0.11","text":"apply_updates! with typed updates (Updates).\nto_dc_pf_instance and the other five constructions.\nsolution[quantity], solution.instance, solution.termination.\nm.producer, m.sources, m.history as typed records.\nnet.detailed_connectivity counts for node breaker sources.","title":"New in 0.11"},{"category":"section","location":"updates/#Updates","page":"Updates","text":"Typed updates change a module in place. Each update names a component by ComponentId, carries an absolute value with an explicit unit, and is applied as part of a batch that is validated as a whole.\n\ncase = parse(\"case9.m\")\nload = ComponentId(\"load\", case.value.loads[1].component_id)   # \"bus-5\"\n\nreport = apply_updates!(case, [\n    set_load_active_power(load, ActivePower(megawatts=91.5)),\n    set_load_reactive_power(load, ReactivePower(megavars=31.0)),\n])\n\nreport.connectivity_changed        # false\nreport.changes[1].component_id     # ComponentId(\"load\", \"bus-5\")\nreport.changes[1].field            # \"load_active_power\"\ncase.value.loads[1].p_mw           # 91.5","title":"Updates"},{"category":"section","location":"updates/#Quantities","page":"Updates","text":"ActivePower, ReactivePower, and ApparentPower take exactly one unit keyword: ActivePower(watts=...) or ActivePower(megawatts=...), ReactivePower(vars=...) or ReactivePower(megavars=...), ApparentPower(volt_amperes=...) or ApparentPower(megavolt_amperes=...).","title":"Quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"updates/#Update-constructors","page":"Updates","text":"Operating point updates:\n\nFunction Field changed\nset_load_active_power, set_load_reactive_power a load's demand\nset_generator_active_power, set_generator_reactive_power a generator's dispatch\nset_generator_voltage_magnitude a generator's voltage setpoint, per unit\nset_generator_in_service, set_branch_in_service service status\nset_transformer_tap_ratio, set_transformer_phase_shift_degrees a transformer branch\nset_switch_closed a switch position\n\nNetwork update: set_branch_thermal_rating. The power setters accept terminal= to address one terminal of a multiconductor element.","title":"Update constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"updates/#Applying-a-batch","page":"Updates","text":"apply_updates! validates every update before changing anything. A batch with an unknown component or an invalid value throws PowerIOError and leaves the module as it was. On success the module's value is refreshed, so case.value reflects the change; a BalancedNetwork obtained before the call keeps the pre-update data. The returned UpdateReport lists every UpdateChange and whether a service status or switch change altered the energized topology.\n\nComponent ids for MATPOWER sources follow the reader's convention: loads and generators are \"bus-N\", branches are \"F-T\". Read the component_id field of the element rather than assuming a convention.","title":"Applying a batch"},{"category":"type","location":"updates/#PowerIO.ActivePower","page":"Updates","text":"ActivePower(; watts) or ActivePower(; megawatts)\n\nActive power with an explicit unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ActivePower"},{"category":"type","location":"updates/#PowerIO.ReactivePower","page":"Updates","text":"ReactivePower(; vars) or ReactivePower(; megavars)\n\nReactive power with an explicit unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ReactivePower"},{"category":"type","location":"updates/#PowerIO.ApparentPower","page":"Updates","text":"ApparentPower(; volt_amperes) or ApparentPower(; megavolt_amperes)\n\nApparent power with an explicit unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.ApparentPower"},{"category":"type","location":"updates/#PowerIO.OperatingPointUpdate","page":"Updates","text":"OperatingPointUpdate\n\nOne change to operating data: a load or generator setpoint, a service status, a transformer tap or phase shift, or a switch position. Construct one with set_load_active_power and the other set_* functions, then apply a batch with apply_updates!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.OperatingPointUpdate"},{"category":"type","location":"updates/#PowerIO.NetworkUpdate","page":"Updates","text":"NetworkUpdate\n\nOne change to network data. set_branch_thermal_rating is the network update in this release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.NetworkUpdate"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_load_active_power","page":"Updates","text":"set_load_active_power(id::ComponentId, power::ActivePower; terminal=nothing)\n\nAn update that sets a load's active power. terminal selects one terminal of a multiconductor load.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_load_active_power"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_load_reactive_power","page":"Updates","text":"set_load_reactive_power(id::ComponentId, power::ReactivePower; terminal=nothing)\n\nAn update that sets a load's reactive power.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_load_reactive_power"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_generator_active_power","page":"Updates","text":"set_generator_active_power(id::ComponentId, power::ActivePower; terminal=nothing)\n\nAn update that sets a generator's active power setpoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_generator_active_power"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_generator_reactive_power","page":"Updates","text":"set_generator_reactive_power(id::ComponentId, power::ReactivePower; terminal=nothing)\n\nAn update that sets a generator's reactive power setpoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_generator_reactive_power"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_generator_voltage_magnitude","page":"Updates","text":"set_generator_voltage_magnitude(id::ComponentId, vm_pu::Real)\n\nAn update that sets a generator's voltage setpoint in per unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_generator_voltage_magnitude"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_generator_in_service","page":"Updates","text":"set_generator_in_service(id::ComponentId, in_service::Bool)\n\nAn update that sets a generator's service status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_generator_in_service"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_branch_in_service","page":"Updates","text":"set_branch_in_service(id::ComponentId, in_service::Bool)\n\nAn update that sets a branch's service status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_branch_in_service"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_transformer_tap_ratio","page":"Updates","text":"set_transformer_tap_ratio(id::ComponentId, tap_ratio::Real)\n\nAn update that sets a transformer branch's tap ratio.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_transformer_tap_ratio"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_transformer_phase_shift_degrees","page":"Updates","text":"set_transformer_phase_shift_degrees(id::ComponentId, degrees::Real)\n\nAn update that sets a transformer branch's phase shift.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_transformer_phase_shift_degrees"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_switch_closed","page":"Updates","text":"set_switch_closed(id::ComponentId, closed::Bool)\n\nAn update that sets a switch position.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_switch_closed"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.set_branch_thermal_rating","page":"Updates","text":"set_branch_thermal_rating(id::ComponentId, rating::ApparentPower; terminal=nothing)\n\nA network update that sets a branch's thermal rating.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_branch_thermal_rating"},{"category":"function","location":"updates/#PowerIO.apply_updates!","page":"Updates","text":"apply_updates!(m::PioModule, updates) -> UpdateReport\n\nValidate the whole batch of OperatingPointUpdate and NetworkUpdate values, then apply it atomically. A failed batch throws PowerIOError and leaves the module unchanged. On success m.value is refreshed; values obtained before the call keep the pre-update data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.apply_updates!"},{"category":"type","location":"updates/#PowerIO.UpdateReport","page":"Updates","text":"UpdateReport\n\nWhat apply_updates! changed: changes::Vector{UpdateChange} and connectivity_changed, true when a service status or switch change altered the energized topology. length(report) is the number of changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.UpdateReport"},{"category":"type","location":"updates/#PowerIO.UpdateChange","page":"Updates","text":"UpdateChange(component_id, field, terminal)\n\nOne change an applied batch made: the component, the field name such as \"load_active_power\" or \"branch_thermal_rating\", and the terminal when the change targeted one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.UpdateChange"},{"category":"section","location":"interop/#Interop","page":"Interop","text":"Target Direction Mechanism\nPowerModels.jl both to_powermodels, from_powermodels through PowerModels JSON\nExaModelsPower.jl out to_powerdata, to_ac_power_data, LoadSeries\nGridFM both parse of a Parquet dataset, emit(m, \"gridfm\")\nPyPSA both parse of a CSV folder, emit(m, \"pypsa-csv\", dir)\nPowerModelsDistribution.jl both emit(m, \"pmd\"), parse of PMD JSON\nBMOPF both emit(m, \"bmopf\"), parse of BMOPF JSON\nOther PowerIO consumers both serialize, deserialize (PowerIO IR)","title":"Interop"},{"category":"section","location":"interop/#PowerModels.jl","page":"Interop","text":"to_powermodels writes the module through the PowerModels JSON writer and returns the network data dictionary PowerModels.jl consumes: per unit powers, radian angles, string keyed component tables. from_powermodels parses such a dictionary (or its JSON text) back into a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}.\n\ncase = parse(\"case14.m\")\ndata = to_powermodels(case)\ndata[\"bus\"][\"1\"][\"bus_type\"]              # 3\nback = from_powermodels(data)             # PioModule{BalancedNetwork}\n\nbuild_powermodels_ref builds the flat reference dictionary (:bus, :gen, :branch, :arcs, :bus_arcs, :ref_buses, ...) that PowerModels models index, and repair_powermodels_angle_bounds! clamps branch angle difference bounds the way PowerModels.correct_voltage_angle_differences! does.","title":"PowerModels.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"interop/#ExaModelsPower.jl","page":"Interop","text":"to_powerdata returns ExaPowerIO's PowerData layout (bus, gen, branch, arc, storage rows with the fields ExaModelsPower reads); to_ac_power_data adds the bound and initial value vectors its build_*_opf functions consume. Powers are per unit, angles are radians, and bus references are positions in the bus table.\n\ndata = to_ac_power_data(\"case118.m\")\ndata.ref_buses\ndata.pmax\n\nPowerIO.LoadSeries builds dense per bus load matrices over several periods for the multiperiod models, from a matrix in MW, a per period multiplier, an id keyed table, or two files.","title":"ExaModelsPower.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"interop/#GridFM","page":"Interop","text":"A GridFM Parquet dataset parses into a ScenarioSet{BalancedNetwork}; each scenario is a network. emit(m, \"gridfm\", dir) writes a dataset. Both need a powerio library built with the gridfm feature; the release binaries include it, and a build without it reports a coded parse error naming the feature.","title":"GridFM"},{"category":"section","location":"interop/#PowerIO-IR","page":"Interop","text":"serialize and deserialize pass a complete module, with its diagnostics and history, to another PowerIO consumer in Rust, Python, Julia, or C. The document shape is \"schema\": \"powerio.module\" and a \"version\" naming the PowerIO release that wrote it; library_version reports this library's own. A library reads the documents of its own compatible release line no newer than itself.","title":"PowerIO IR"},{"category":"function","location":"interop/#PowerIO.to_powermodels","page":"Interop","text":"to_powermodels(m::PioModule{BalancedNetwork}) -> Dict{String,Any}\nto_powermodels(net::BalancedNetwork) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe network as a PowerModels network data dictionary, written by the PowerIO PowerModels JSON writer: per unit powers, radian angles, string keyed component tables. A bare BalancedNetwork works when it came straight from a module; a network reached through a collection or an instance has no module of its own, so pass the module instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_powermodels"},{"category":"function","location":"interop/#PowerIO.from_powermodels","page":"Interop","text":"from_powermodels(data) -> PioModule{BalancedNetwork}\n\nParse PowerModels network data, given as a dictionary, a NamedTuple, or a JSON string, into a network module.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.from_powermodels"},{"category":"function","location":"interop/#PowerIO.build_powermodels_ref","page":"Interop","text":"build_powermodels_ref(network_data::Dict) -> Dict{Symbol,Any}\n\nThe flat equivalent of PowerModels.build_ref(data)[:it][:pm][:nw][0] restricted to:\n\n:bus, :gen, :branch, :load, :shunt: integer keyed component tables filtered to active elements on active buses (bus_type != 4, status fields nonzero, live endpoints);\n:arcs_from, :arcs_to, :arcs: (branch_id, from_bus, to_bus) tuples;\n:bus_arcs, :bus_gens, :bus_loads, :bus_shunts: per bus index lists;\n:ref_buses: buses with bus_type == 3;\n:baseMVA.\n\nStorage, switches, DC lines, and areas are not carried. Branch angle bounds in ref[:branch] are repaired with repair_powermodels_angle_bounds! on copies; every other table shares its rows with network_data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.build_powermodels_ref"},{"category":"function","location":"interop/#PowerIO.repair_powermodels_angle_bounds!","page":"Interop","text":"repair_powermodels_angle_bounds!(network_data::Dict; default_pad=1.0472)\n\nClamp every branch angle difference bound in a PowerModels network data dictionary to [-default_pad, default_pad] radians, the correction PowerModels.correct_voltage_angle_differences! applies: a lower bound at or below -pi/2 becomes -default_pad, an upper bound at or above pi/2 becomes default_pad, and a zero width band opens to the full pad. Returns the dictionary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.repair_powermodels_angle_bounds!"},{"category":"function","location":"interop/#PowerIO.to_powerdata","page":"Interop","text":"to_powerdata(net; T=Float64) -> NamedTuple\nto_powerdata(m::PioModule{BalancedNetwork}; T=Float64) -> NamedTuple\nto_powerdata(path; format=nothing, T=Float64) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe network in ExaPowerIO's PowerData layout: version, baseMVA, bus, gen, branch, arc, and storage, with the row fields ExaModelsPower reads. Powers are per unit on baseMVA, angles are radians, bus_i keeps the source bus id, and branch and generator bus references are 1-based positions in bus. Bus types are reconciled with the generator table: a bus with an in-service generator is at least PV, a bus without one is PQ, and when no bus is the reference the bus with the largest generator becomes it.\n\nA field may be Inf only where a source format spells an absent limit that way: generator and storage power limits, branch ratings, and angle difference bounds. Every other non-finite value is refused with the element and field named.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_powerdata"},{"category":"function","location":"interop/#PowerIO.to_ac_power_data","page":"Interop","text":"to_ac_power_data(net; T=Float64) -> NamedTuple\nto_ac_power_data(m::PioModule{BalancedNetwork}; T=Float64) -> NamedTuple\nto_ac_power_data(path; format=nothing, T=Float64) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe layout ExaModelsPower's build_polar_opf, build_rect_opf, and build_dcopf consume: the to_powerdata tables plus the bound and initial value vectors they read (ref_buses, vmax, vmin, pmax, pmin, qmax, qmin, angmax, angmin, rate_a, vm0, va0, pg0, qg0, and the storage ratings).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.to_ac_power_data"},{"category":"type","location":"interop/#PowerIO.LoadSeries","page":"Interop","text":"LoadSeries{T}\n\nDense per bus loads over several periods for ExaModelsPower's multiperiod OPF. pd and qd are n_buses by n_periods matrices per unit on base_mva, ordered like the network's bus table; bus_ids[k] is the source id of row k.\n\nPowerIO.LoadSeries(net, pd_mw, qd_mw)           # rows are buses in network order, MW\nPowerIO.LoadSeries(net, curve)                  # scale the base case loads per period\nPowerIO.LoadSeries(net, pd_by_id, qd_by_id)     # Dict(bus_id => per period MW vector)\nPowerIO.read_load_series(net, pd_path, qd_path) # the matrix form, from two files\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.LoadSeries"},{"category":"function","location":"interop/#PowerIO.n_periods","page":"Interop","text":"n_periods(series::LoadSeries) -> Int\n\nNumber of periods in a LoadSeries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.n_periods"},{"category":"function","location":"interop/#PowerIO.demands_mw","page":"Interop","text":"demands_mw(series::LoadSeries) -> (; pd, qd)\n\nThe demand matrices in MW: series.pd .* series.base_mva and the same for qd.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.demands_mw"},{"category":"function","location":"interop/#PowerIO.read_load_series","page":"Interop","text":"read_load_series(net::BalancedNetwork, pd_path, qd_path; T=Float64)\n\nRead two whitespace delimited MW load matrices (rows are buses in net's order, columns are periods) into a LoadSeries. The files hold MW; the series holds per unit; demands_mw converts back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.read_load_series"},{"category":"section","location":"binary/#Binary-distribution","page":"Binary distribution","text":"PowerIO.jl wraps the Rust powerio-capi library, shipped as a prebuilt binary for each platform; users never compile it.","title":"Binary distribution"},{"category":"section","location":"binary/#Pipeline","page":"Binary distribution","text":"A version tag on eigenergy/powerio triggers its release-binaries workflow, which builds libpowerio_capi.<triplet>.tar.gz with the arrow, matrix, gridfm, dist, and prob features for Linux glibc (x86_64, aarch64), macOS (x86_64, arm64), and Windows (x86_64), and attaches the five tarballs to the GitHub release. Each tarball holds the library under lib/ (bin/ on Windows), the C header under include/, and the licenses.\nA reviewed .github/powerio-release.toml names the exact tag and binds it to the final Julia source digest. \"Update artifacts\" downloads the five tarballs, computes each one's SHA-256 and unpacked git tree hash, and asks gen/update_artifacts.jl to build Artifacts.toml. Before writing, the generator loads the host library and checks its ABI number (7), its version string against the tag, the core entry points the binding calls, and that it parses the GridFM fixture. A mismatch parks the update with Artifacts.toml untouched. The workflow tests the exact result, commits only Artifacts.toml, pushes only when main has not moved, and dispatches registration for that exact SHA. See CONTRIBUTING.md for the state machine and recovery commands.\nArtifacts.toml is lazy: nothing downloads at Pkg.add; the tarball for the current platform is fetched on the first call that needs the library.","title":"Pipeline"},{"category":"section","location":"binary/#Resolution-order","page":"Binary distribution","text":"The library resolves in this order:\n\nset_library!(path) or the POWERIO_CAPI environment variable,\nthe saved Preferences.jl library override,\na sibling powerio checkout's target/{release,debug} build,\nthe powerio_capi artifact,\na plain libpowerio_capi on the loader path.\n\nOn an unsupported platform the artifact lookup fails and the fallbacks keep a local build working.\n\nusing PowerIO\n\nset_library!(\"/path/to/libpowerio_capi.dylib\"; persist=true)\nclear_library!(persist=true)","title":"Resolution order"},{"category":"section","location":"binary/#JLL","page":"Binary distribution","text":"A PowerIO_jll built from gen/build_tarballs.jl (the BinaryBuilder recipe) is the planned long term distribution; current releases use the artifact pipeline above.","title":"JLL"},{"category":"function","location":"binary/#PowerIO.set_library!","page":"Binary distribution","text":"set_library!(path; persist=false)\n\nPoint PowerIO at a locally built libpowerio_capi (cargo build -p powerio-capi --release in the powerio Rust tree writes target/release/libpowerio_capi.{dylib,so}). An in-session override wins over POWERIO_CAPI, the saved Preferences.jl override, and the bundled artifact. Pass persist=true to save the path in the active environment's LocalPreferences.toml.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.set_library!"},{"category":"function","location":"binary/#PowerIO.clear_library!","page":"Binary distribution","text":"clear_library!(; persist=false)\n\nClear the in-session library override. Pass persist=true to also clear the saved Preferences.jl library override. POWERIO_CAPI, when set, still wins on this session's next call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.clear_library!"},{"category":"function","location":"binary/#PowerIO.abi_version","page":"Binary distribution","text":"abi_version() -> UInt32\n\nThe ABI version the resolved C library was built with (see pio_abi_version). Compared against PIO_ABI_VERSION, the version this binding targets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.abi_version"},{"category":"function","location":"binary/#PowerIO.library_version","page":"Binary distribution","text":"library_version() -> String\n\nThe powerio crate version string the resolved library reports, such as \"0.11.0\". Informational; abi_version is the compatibility check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.library_version"},{"category":"function","location":"binary/#PowerIO.library_available","page":"Binary distribution","text":"library_available() -> Bool\n\nTrue if the C ABI library resolves and is ABI compatible with this binding (see abi_version).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.library_available"},{"category":"section","location":"matrices/#Matrices","page":"Matrices","text":"Two groups of calculations take a BalancedNetwork or a PioModule{BalancedNetwork} and return SparseMatrixCSC or Vector{Float64} values with 1-based indices.","title":"Matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"matrices/#DC-calculations","page":"Matrices","text":"The eight DC calculations are computed by the powerio library and share their names with the Rust, Python, and C APIs. With A the branch by bus incidence matrix (+1 at the from bus, -1 at the to bus of every in-service branch) and b the branch susceptances:\n\nA  = calc_incidence_matrix(net)            # branches by buses\nb  = calc_branch_susceptances(net)         # one per in-service branch\nB  = calc_bus_susceptance_matrix(net)      # A' * Diagonal(b) * A\nBf = calc_branch_flow_matrix(net)          # Diagonal(b) * A\nps = calc_branch_phase_shift_injection(net)\npb = calc_bus_phase_shift_injection(net)\n\nva = zeros(length(net.buses))              # bus voltage angles, radians\npf = calc_branch_flow_dc(net, va)          # -(Bf * va) + ps\np  = calc_bus_injection_dc(net, va)        # -(B * va) + pb\n\nformula=\"series_susceptance\" (the default) uses the imaginary part of the series admittance as the branch susceptance. Rows follow the bus and branch tables of the network.","title":"DC calculations"},{"category":"section","location":"matrices/#Admittance-matrices","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_admittance_matrix assembles the complex bus admittance matrix Y = G + jB in Julia from the element tables, following MATPOWER's makeYbus as the powerio matrix crate implements it. For each in-service branch from bus i to bus j with series impedance z = r + jx, terminal charging y_fr and y_to, and complex tap a = tap * exp(j * shift):\n\nY[i,i] += (1/z + y_fr) / |a|^2\nY[j,j] += 1/z + y_to\nY[i,j] += -(1/z) / conj(a)\nY[j,i] += -(1/z) / a\n\nplus the in-service bus shunts Y[i,i] += (g_s + j b_s) / base_mva. The result is a BusMappedMatrix with idx_to_bus, bus_to_idx, and the sparse matrix.\n\nY = calc_admittance_matrix(net)\nY.matrix[Y.bus_to_idx[4], Y.bus_to_idx[5]]\ncalc_admittance_matrix(net; include_taps=false)\ncalc_admittance_matrix(\"case9.m\")          # parse, then assemble\n\ncalc_bprime_matrix and calc_bdoubleprime_matrix are the fast decoupled B' and B'' matrices: the same kernel with charging, taps, shifts, shunts, and series resistance switched as MATPOWER's makeB does, negated. scheme=:bx (the default) or :xb selects where the series resistance is dropped.\n\nA branch whose impedance magnitude is below the divisibility threshold is an error unless skip_zero_impedance=true drops it.","title":"Admittance matrices"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_incidence_matrix","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_incidence_matrix(net; formula=\"series_susceptance\") -> SparseMatrixCSC\n\nBranch by bus incidence matrix: +1 at the from bus and -1 at the to bus of every in-service branch. formula selects the branch susceptance: \"series_susceptance\" (the imaginary part of the series admittance, the default) or another formula the library accepts. Row and column order follow the bus and branch tables; indices are 1-based. The argument is a BalancedNetwork or a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_incidence_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_branch_susceptances","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_branch_susceptances(net; formula=\"series_susceptance\") -> Vector{Float64}\n\nOne susceptance per in-service branch. formula selects the branch susceptance: \"series_susceptance\" (the imaginary part of the series admittance, the default) or another formula the library accepts. Row and column order follow the bus and branch tables; indices are 1-based. The argument is a BalancedNetwork or a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_branch_susceptances"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_bus_susceptance_matrix","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_bus_susceptance_matrix(net; formula=\"series_susceptance\") -> SparseMatrixCSC\n\nThe DC bus susceptance matrix A' * Diagonal(b) * A. formula selects the branch susceptance: \"series_susceptance\" (the imaginary part of the series admittance, the default) or another formula the library accepts. Row and column order follow the bus and branch tables; indices are 1-based. The argument is a BalancedNetwork or a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_bus_susceptance_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_branch_flow_matrix","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_branch_flow_matrix(net; formula=\"series_susceptance\") -> SparseMatrixCSC\n\nThe branch flow matrix Diagonal(b) * A, mapping bus angles to branch flows. formula selects the branch susceptance: \"series_susceptance\" (the imaginary part of the series admittance, the default) or another formula the library accepts. Row and column order follow the bus and branch tables; indices are 1-based. The argument is a BalancedNetwork or a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_branch_flow_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_branch_phase_shift_injection","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_branch_phase_shift_injection(net; formula=\"series_susceptance\") -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe per branch injection caused by transformer phase shifts. formula selects the branch susceptance: \"series_susceptance\" (the imaginary part of the series admittance, the default) or another formula the library accepts. Row and column order follow the bus and branch tables; indices are 1-based. The argument is a BalancedNetwork or a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_branch_phase_shift_injection"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_bus_phase_shift_injection","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_bus_phase_shift_injection(net; formula=\"series_susceptance\") -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe per bus injection caused by transformer phase shifts. formula selects the branch susceptance: \"series_susceptance\" (the imaginary part of the series admittance, the default) or another formula the library accepts. Row and column order follow the bus and branch tables; indices are 1-based. The argument is a BalancedNetwork or a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_bus_phase_shift_injection"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_branch_flow_dc","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_branch_flow_dc(net, voltage_angles; formula=\"series_susceptance\") -> Vector{Float64}\n\nDC branch flows in per unit for the given bus voltage angles in radians, one per bus in table order. formula selects the branch susceptance: \"series_susceptance\" (the imaginary part of the series admittance, the default) or another formula the library accepts. Row and column order follow the bus and branch tables; indices are 1-based. The argument is a BalancedNetwork or a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_branch_flow_dc"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_bus_injection_dc","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_bus_injection_dc(net, voltage_angles; formula=\"series_susceptance\") -> Vector{Float64}\n\nDC bus injections in per unit for the given bus voltage angles in radians. formula selects the branch susceptance: \"series_susceptance\" (the imaginary part of the series admittance, the default) or another formula the library accepts. Row and column order follow the bus and branch tables; indices are 1-based. The argument is a BalancedNetwork or a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_bus_injection_dc"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_admittance_matrix","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_admittance_matrix(net; include_taps=true, include_shifts=true, skip_zero_impedance=false)\n\nThe complex bus admittance matrix Y = G + jB in per unit on the system base, following MATPOWER's makeYbus: series admittances, terminal charging, transformer taps and phase shifts, and in-service bus shunts. A zero impedance branch is an error unless skip_zero_impedance=true drops it. Returns a BusMappedMatrix. The argument is a BalancedNetwork, a PioModule{BalancedNetwork}, or a path to parse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_admittance_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_bprime_matrix","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_bprime_matrix(net; scheme=:bx, skip_zero_impedance=false)\n\nMATPOWER's fast decoupled B' matrix: the negated imaginary part of a bus admittance matrix built with unity taps, no line charging, no bus shunts, and phase shifts kept. scheme=:xb also drops series resistance. Returns a real BusMappedMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_bprime_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.calc_bdoubleprime_matrix","page":"Matrices","text":"calc_bdoubleprime_matrix(net; scheme=:bx, skip_zero_impedance=false)\n\nMATPOWER's fast decoupled B'' matrix: the negated imaginary part of a bus admittance matrix built with phase shifts cleared and taps, charging, and bus shunts kept. scheme=:bx (the default) also drops series resistance. Returns a real BusMappedMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.calc_bdoubleprime_matrix"},{"category":"type","location":"matrices/#PowerIO.BusMappedMatrix","page":"Matrices","text":"BusMappedMatrix{T}\n\nA sparse matrix over the bus table with its index maps: idx_to_bus[k] is the bus id of row and column k, bus_to_idx[id] the row of bus id, and matrix the SparseMatrixCSC{T,Int}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PowerIO.BusMappedMatrix"},{"category":"section","location":"memory-safety/#Memory-safety","page":"Memory safety","text":"PowerIO.jl holds memory owned by the Rust library only through handle objects, and copies every value it hands to Julia code.","title":"Memory safety"},{"category":"section","location":"memory-safety/#Handles","page":"Memory safety","text":"Each opaque C handle (PioModule, PioBalancedNetwork, PioTimeSeries, PioEmitResult, PioSparseMatrix, and the rest) is wrapped by one mutable Julia handle type with a finalizer that calls the family's release function. The release function is resolved from the library that allocated the pointer before the handle is constructed, so a set_library! switch after allocation never releases a pointer through the wrong library. Every C call that takes a handle runs inside GC.@preserve of that handle.","title":"Handles"},{"category":"section","location":"memory-safety/#Values-that-keep-their-module-alive","page":"Memory safety","text":"A network, collection, instance, or solution borrowed from a module holds a reference to the module's data inside the library. Releasing the module handle does not invalidate the borrowed handle:\n\nnet = parse(\"case9.m\").value   # the PioModule is unreachable after this line\nGC.gc()\nlength(net.buses)              # still 9\n\nEntries read from a TimeSeries or ScenarioSet and the network of an instance work the same way.","title":"Values that keep their module alive"},{"category":"section","location":"memory-safety/#Copies","page":"Memory safety","text":"Element structs, diagnostics, module records, emitted file contents, sparse matrices, and vectors are copied out of the borrowed C views before the call returns; none of them points into library memory. A Bus read from net.buses[1] stays valid after the network is released. The cost is one allocation per element read; collect(net.buses) materializes a table once.","title":"Copies"},{"category":"section","location":"memory-safety/#Updates","page":"Memory safety","text":"apply_updates! changes a module in place. The library detaches the module's data before a successful change, so a BalancedNetwork obtained before the call keeps the old data and m.value is refreshed to the new one.","title":"Updates"},{"category":"section","location":"memory-safety/#Threads","page":"Memory safety","text":"Concurrent reads of one handle are allowed by the library. Releasing a handle while another task uses it is a caller error; the Julia wrappers do not add a lock, so share handles between tasks only for reads.","title":"Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"languages/#Language-map","page":"Language map","text":"The Rust, Python, Julia, and C APIs share one vocabulary. The complete table is in the powerio documentation (Rust, Python, Julia, and C); this page states what is specific to Julia.\n\nMeaning Julia Python C ABI 7\nacquire a path parse(\"case.m\") parse(Path(\"case.m\")) pio_source_open\nacquire memory parse(io; name), parse(bytes; name) pass a file or bytes-like object pio_source_from_memory\nparse parse(source; format) parse(source, format=...) pio_parse\nmodule value m.value module.value pio_module_value\nmodule diagnostics m.diagnostics module.diagnostics pio_module_diagnostics\nemit a format emit(m, format, destination) emit(module, format, destination) pio_emit\nserialize IR serialize(m, destination) serialize(module, destination) pio_module_serialize\ndeserialize IR deserialize(source) deserialize(source) pio_module_deserialize\napply updates apply_updates!(m, updates) apply_updates(module, updates) pio_apply_updates\nbus table net.buses (Elements{Bus}) network.buses (list of dicts) pio_balanced_network_bus_count, pio_balanced_network_bus_at\ntime series entry series[1] series[0] pio_time_series_get(series, 0)\nscenario entry scenarios[\"7\"] scenarios[\"7\"] pio_scenario_set_get","title":"Language map"},{"category":"section","location":"languages/#Julia-specifics","page":"Language map","text":"parse extends Base.parse with methods for a path string, an IO, and a byte vector. No Base method takes one positional argument, so existing code is unaffected, and the bare name works after using PowerIO.\nType discovery is dispatch: PioModule{BalancedNetwork}, PioModule{TimeSeries{BalancedNetwork}}, and so on. There is no kind enum.\nTables are properties returning AbstractVectors of immutable structs. Field names follow the C header (vm_pu, active_power_mw); Python uses the MATPOWER short names (vm, pg).\nIndices are 1-based: collection entries, sparse matrix positions, and the to_dense bus rows. Bus ids and component ids are source identifiers and do not change at the language boundary.\nErrors are PowerIOError exceptions carrying the code, message, and diagnostics; Python raises PowerIOError subclasses; C writes one PioError.\nMutating operations end in !: apply_updates!, set_library!, repair_powermodels_angle_bounds!.\nOnly Rust and C have a Source type, because those languages need an explicit owner for acquired bytes. A Julia path, IO, or byte vector already states where the bytes come from and who owns them.\nThe admittance matrices (calc_admittance_matrix, calc_bprime_matrix, calc_bdoubleprime_matrix) are assembled in Julia; Python reaches the Rust implementation directly. The eight DC calculations come from the library in every language.","title":"Julia specifics"},{"category":"section","location":"#PowerIO.jl","page":"Home","text":"PowerIO.jl is the Julia binding of PowerIO, a compiler for power system data. It reads grid exchange formats (MATPOWER, PSS/E RAW and RAWX, XIIDM, CGMES, PowerWorld, PSLF, PyPSA, GridFM, OpenDSS, PMD JSON, BMOPF, and the PowerModels, Egret, and pandapower JSON dialects) into typed Julia values, writes them back out, and computes the matrices power flow and optimization code needs.\n\nusing Pkg; Pkg.add(\"PowerIO\")\n\nusing PowerIO\n\ncase = parse(\"case9.m\")              # PioModule{BalancedNetwork}\nnet = case.value\nlength(net.buses)                    # 9\nnet.branches[1].reactance_pu         # 0.0576\n[g.active_power_mw for g in net.generators]\ncase.diagnostics                     # what the reader kept, defaulted, or dropped\n\nemit(case, \"matpower\", \"copy.m\")     # same format: the original file, unchanged\nresult = emit(case, \"psse\")          # another format, in memory\nresult.text\nresult.diagnostics                   # what PSS/E cannot carry\n\nserialize(case, \"case9.pio.json\")    # PowerIO IR, for other PowerIO consumers","title":"PowerIO.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-vocabulary","page":"Home","text":"Five operations cover the binding:\n\nOperation What it does\nparse(source; format) reads one grid exchange source into a PioModule{T}\nemit(module, format, destination) writes a grid exchange format\nserialize(module, destination) and deserialize(source) move PowerIO IR between PowerIO consumers\ncalc_*(net) computes a matrix or vector\nto_*(module) constructs another value type in memory\napply_updates!(module, updates) changes a module in place\n\nElement tables are properties of a network (net.buses, net.branches, net.lines) and behave as Julia vectors of immutable structs. Indices are 1-based everywhere. parse extends Base.parse, so the bare name works after using PowerIO.","title":"The vocabulary"},{"category":"section","location":"#Pages","page":"Home","text":"Modules: PioModule, sources, diagnostics, emit, PowerIO IR.\nNetworks: BalancedNetwork and its element structs.\nDistribution: MulticonductorNetwork.\nCollections and instances: time series, scenario sets, calculation instances and solutions.\nMatrices: the DC calculations and the admittance matrices.\nUpdates: typed updates and apply_updates!.\nInterop: PowerModels.jl, ExaModelsPower, GridFM.\nDeveloper guides: migrating to 0.11, the binary distribution, memory safety, the language map, and the API reference.","title":"Pages"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-C-library","page":"Home","text":"PowerIO.jl calls the powerio C library (libpowerio_capi, C ABI 7). The library resolves automatically: the bundled artifact on a supported platform, or a sibling powerio checkout's build during development. Point at another build with set_library! or the POWERIO_CAPI environment variable. library_available reports whether a compatible library resolved and abi_version the ABI number it was built with.","title":"The C library"}]
}
